# docs-check convergence (fixed point) - why the health block needs ONE FOLLOW-UP commit
# date: 2026-11-12   cwd: E:\ComputerScience\epos_Zht\epos_Zht

## what happened
The 4th health block records, per inner doc, the date of that file's LAST GIT COMMIT.
Before committing, those dates are the OLD ones (2026-11-11 for decisions-autodev.md,
(mtime) for the newly created docs/decisions-docs.md, 2026-10-28 for README.md).
Committing the round changes exactly those dates to today - which makes the block that
was just committed stale, so the very next 'make docs-check' fails C7.

## observed
eec6d01  docs: the refactor itself (22 files)
  -> uv run --no-project python tools/check_docs.py
     FAIL C7  progress.html:health -> block differs from --sync output (22 vs 22 lines)
     RESULT FAIL (1 fail / 1 warn)
  -> uv run --no-project python tools/check_docs.py --sync
     changed 4 rows: decisions-autodev.md 2026-11-11 -> 2026-11-12
                     decisions-docs.md   2026-11-12 (mtime) -> 2026-11-12
                     README.md            2026-10-28 -> 2026-11-12
3609947  docs: refresh generated health block after first commit (1 file, 3 +-)
  -> uv run --no-project python tools/check_docs.py --sync
     no diff  <- the block is now a FIXED POINT

## rule for future rounds (D-D6 step 5)
Commit, then run '--sync' again.  If it rewrites the block, commit that as a second
small commit.  Do NOT try to make it converge inside one commit - it cannot, by
construction.  And never 'fix' it by writing a dirty flag into the block: that was
tried and it breaks on every single commit instead of once.

